**Part 1: LRU cache.** Use a `HashMap<K, Node>` plus a doubly linked list ordered by recency. Keep sentinel `head` and `tail` nodes to avoid null checks.

```java
class LRUCache<K,V> {
  class Node { K k; V v; Node prev, next; }
  Map<K,Node> map = new HashMap<>();
  Node head = new Node(), tail = new Node();
  int cap;
  V get(K k) {
    Node n = map.get(k); if (n == null) return null;
    unlink(n); addFront(n); return n.v;
  }
  void put(K k, V v) {
    Node n = map.get(k);
    if (n != null) { n.v = v; unlink(n); addFront(n); return; }
    if (map.size() == cap) { Node lru = tail.prev; unlink(lru); map.remove(lru.k); }
    n = new Node(); n.k = k; n.v = v; map.put(k, n); addFront(n);
  }
}
```

`get` and `put` are O(1). The node stores its key so eviction can remove the map entry. For thread safety, say you would use a lock (or `synchronized`) around both operations, since `get` mutates the list; a `ConcurrentHashMap` alone is not enough. In Java, `LinkedHashMap` with access order and `removeEldestEntry` is the shortcut, but implement it by hand if asked. Make the eviction policy an interface if LFU might follow.

